messages.content:
When you are matching one value against several patterns — especially glob patterns — case says it in the shape of the problem:

```bash
case "$file" in
    *.tar.gz|*.tgz) tar -xzf "$file" ;;
    *.zip)           unzip "$file" ;;
    *.gz)            gunzip "$file" ;;
    *)               echo "unknown type: $file" >&2; exit 1 ;;
esac
```

Reading notes: patterns are globs, not strings and not regex — `*.tar.gz|*.tgz` means "either alternative", and order matters because the *first* matching arm wins (which is why the two-extension pattern precedes plain `*.gz`: a .tar.gz also matches `*.gz`, and arms are tried top to bottom). Each arm ends with `;;`; the final `*)` arm is the default, and in dispatch code it should almost always exist — silently falling through on an unrecognized value is how files get skipped without a trace.

Versus if/elif: a chain of `[ "$x" = ... ]` tests re-evaluates the variable each time, cannot express glob matching without `[[ == ]]` gymnastics per branch, and visually buries the fact that it is a dispatch table. Reach for if when the conditions involve *different* variables, numeric ranges, file tests, or compound logic; reach for case when one value is being routed. Bash also offers `;&` (fall through into the next arm's body) and `;;&` (continue testing later arms) — real tools, but use them sparingly; the plain `;;` table is the one the next reader will parse at a glance.

arguments.code:
for f in report.pdf photo.jpg notes.txt song.mp3 data.csv; do
  case "$f" in
    *.txt|*.md) kind=text ;;
    *.jpg|*.png) kind=image ;;
    *.pdf) kind=document ;;
    *) kind=other ;;
  esac
  echo "$f -> $kind"
done
